(def assoc-page "(defmacro assoc (value key more ...))\n\nReturns a struct or a collection with new values at its keys.\n\nA keyword key sets a field, as in `(assoc point :x 1 :y 2)`. An unknown\nfield is an error, and a keyword without a value is an error. The call moves\neach field it does not list, unless the field is `copy`.\n\nAny other key updates a vector or a map, as in `(assoc items 0 9)`. Keys and\nvalues come in pairs, and each pair updates the result of the one before.\nThe call moves the collection.\n\nA vector index is a `usize`, so an `i64` is an error, and the call panics\nwhen the index is out of bounds. A map key has the type of the map's keys: a\nstring literal is a `(ref str)`, so a `string` key takes `(str \"name\")`,\nand a `(ref str)` key takes a literal or `(as-str name)`. A map adds a\nmissing key.")
(def assoc-in-page "(defmacro assoc-in (value (step ...) item))\n\nReturns a struct or a collection with a new item at a path of steps.\n\nA keyword step names a field, as in `(assoc-in line (:start :x) 3)`, and the\ncall moves each field the path does not change, unless it is `copy`.\n\nAn index step names a vector element, as in `(assoc-in grid (1 0) 7)`. An\nindex is a `usize`, so an `i64` is an error, and the call panics when it is\nout of bounds. A keyword step after an index step is an error.\n\nA map key step names a map's value, as in `(assoc-in scores (\"ana\") 7)` or\n`(assoc-in team (:scores \"ana\") 7)`, and adds a missing key. It is the\nlast step, and it follows only keyword steps. Its key has the type of the\nmap's keys: a string literal is a `(ref str)`, so a `string` key takes\n`(str \"name\")`, and a `(ref str)` key takes a literal or `(as-str name)`.\n\nA path of only index or map key steps moves the collection.")
(def set-page "(defmacro set! (target value))\n\nChanges a local, a field or an element in place.\n\nThe target is a name, a field read such as `(:x (:start line))`, an `at`\nform such as `(at (:items order) 0)`, or a `(mut a)`. A field of an `at`\nform, as in `(:x (at points 0))`, is an error, and so is any other target.\n\nAn `at` index is a `usize`, so an `i64` is an error, and a vector panics\nwhen the index is out of bounds. A map panics on a missing key. A map key\nmust fit the map's key type: a `(ref str)` key takes a literal or\n`(as-str name)`, and a `string` is an error.")
